Before `new;`, a constructor may no longer read a `const` field whose initializer depends on `this` (by naming another field, another such constant, an instance function, or `this` itself). The verifier gave such a read the constant's final value, the initializer evaluated on the fields' values at the end of the first phase, while the fields could still change: with `const s: D := t`, the assignment `t := One(s)` made `s == t` and `t == One(s)` both hold and proved `false`. Such a read is now a resolution error. Constants whose initializers do not mention `this` can still be read before `new;`, and every constant can be read after it. The hidden option `--legacy-first-phase-const-reads` restores the old, unsound rule, for comparison and migration only.
